# r_metal_Pauling.dat -- metallic valences and radii of the elements, Pauling (1960) Table 11-1.
#
# CITE:  Pauling, L. (1960). The Nature of the Chemical Bond and the Structure
#        of Molecules and Crystals, 3rd ed. Cornell University Press, Ithaca NY.
#        Table 11-1, "Metallic valences and radii of the elements", p. 403.
#        Check: Pauling, L. (1947). Atomic radii and interatomic distances in
#        metals. J. Am. Chem. Soc. 69, 542-553. doi:10.1021/ja01195a024
#        (Table I, pp. 544-545).
#
# STATUS: every number below is Pauling's, transcribed for this package from
# the printed page; no file of anyone else's was copied. Three cells are
# corrected misprints (CORRECTIONS below); every other value is as printed.
# Cite the book, never this file or a copy, as the source of a radius.
#
# WHY TABLE 11-1 AND NOT TABLE 7-18. The book prints two R(1) tables.
# Table 7-18, "Single-bond metallic radii" (p. 256, 49 elements, R(1) only),
# is essentially the 1947 set (d-block 1947 values + 0.005 A; Cu-Pb as in
# 1947). Table 11-1 (p. 403, 72 elements) carries the 1960 revision of the
# valences and radii and is the only table with v and R(L12), Sb, Bi and the
# lanthanides. They differ on 16 of the 49 shared elements, most at Tl
# (0.112 A), In (0.076), Hg (0.054), Zn and Pb (0.036). Table 11-1 is used.
#
# TRANSCRIPTION (2026-10-02). Read cell by cell from 400-800 dpi images of
# two copies of the 3rd edition (the Cornell fifth printing, 1964, and the
# Oxford & IBH 1967 reprint of the fourth printing, 1963); both carry the
# same misprints, so they come from the same plates. 72 entries, 216 cells,
# in printed order: Li Be B / Na-S / K-Se / Rb-Te / Cs-Bi / Th U / Ce-Lu.
#
# CHECKS, with counts.
#   1. Two machine readings of the table (one per copy) were compared with
#      the page cell by cell; 112 disagreements, every one decided by the
#      page image, and the page confirmed this transcription each time.
#   2. Pauling's own relation between the two radius columns: for ligancy
#      12 the change to bond number 1 is "0.600 log (v/12)" on the distance
#      (p. 412; Eq. 11-1, p. 400: D(n) = D(1) - 0.600 log n), so
#      R(L12) = R1 + 0.300 log(12/v). As printed it holds within the printed
#      precision on 69 of 72 rows; the three breaks are Gd, Eu and Pm, below.
#      After the two corrections it holds on 71 of 72 (Pm, kept as printed).
#   3. The 1947 table: 32 of 72 R1 values unchanged, 38 revised in 1960, two
#      (Tc, Pm) with no 1947 radius. Each revision was read on both pages.
#
# CORRECTIONS (column `as_printed` holds the printed cell; the value in the
# row is the corrected one):
#   Gd  R(L12) printed 2.804, corrected to 1.804. The relation gives
#       1.623 + 0.300 log(12/3) = 1.804; Sm, with the same v = 3 and
#       R1 = 1.623, prints 1.804; the 1947 table gives Gd R(CN12) 1.795
#       (R1 1.614), also with leading digit 1. A slip of the leading digit.
#   Eu  v printed 3, corrected to 2. The printed radii are right and imply
#       v = 1.99 by the relation (2.084 - 1.850 = 0.234 = 0.300 log(12/v));
#       the 1947 table prints Eu v = 2 with the same two radii, a valence
#       taken there from Klemm & Bommer's lattice constants (1947, pp.
#       545-546). The radii are as printed.
#   Sr  the cell is labelled "Sn" in both copies. It sits in Sr's place
#       (between Rb and Y) and its values, v 2, R(L12) 2.148, R1 1.914, are
#       the 1947 table's Sr. Sn is printed separately, in its own place.
# FLAGGED, NOT CORRECTED:
#   Pm  R(L12) printed 1.834; the relation gives 1.633 + 0.300 log 4 = 1.814
#       (residual +0.020 A). R1 1.633 sits on the Nd-Sm trend, so R(L12) is
#       the suspect cell, but Pm has no 1947 entry and the page cannot decide
#       it. Kept as printed, with the flag r12_off_relation.
#
# WHAT THEY ARE: R1 is "the metallic radius" (p. 404), the radius for a bond
# of bond number 1 with "the same hybrid character as in the metals
# themselves" (p. 256); R(L12) is "the metallic radius for ligancy 12" (p.
# 404); v is "the assumed values of the metallic valence" (p. 404). Radii for
# metal-metal contacts and for boride-type frameworks that Pauling treats
# with the same scheme (pp. 365-366), in intermetallic and anion-free phases;
# not ionic radii, and not this package's bond table, which uses Cordero et
# al. (2008) through gemmi (model/geometry.py).
#
# COLUMNS:
#   symbol      element symbol (the printed label, except the corrected Sr)
#   v           metallic valence as used here (Eu corrected); a value printed
#               in parentheses (P, S, Se, Te) is flagged v_assumed
#   r12_A       R(L12), A, with the printed decimals (Gd corrected)
#   r1_A        R1, A, with the printed decimals
#   page        printed page of the table
#   flags       - or comma-separated: v_assumed, v_corrected, r12_corrected,
#               label_corrected, r12_off_relation
#   as_printed  - or the printed cell(s) a correction replaced
#
# symbol  v  r12_A  r1_A  page  flags  as_printed
